      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "modern_form": "PIE *meuk- 'to scratch, tear' (Pokorny 745; = *h₂mewḱ- in the modern notation, on the strength of Gk. ἀμύσσω / ἀμυκαλαί)",
      "note": "Pokorny IEW 745 really does have a root 'meuk-' with the meaning 'kratzen, reißen' = 'to scratch, tear'; the language label (Indo-European) and the page are exact. Nourai's gloss 'scratch, rub' renders 'reißen' ('tear') as 'rub', which is the one inaccuracy. Pokorny's whole material is: Gk. ἀμυκαλαί 'arrow-tips' (Hes.), ἀμύσσω / Att. ἀμύττω 'I split, scratch', ἀμυχή 'scratch'; Lat. mucrō; 'perhaps' OE ge-myscan and Lith. mušti 'hit'; 'perhaps also' OInd. muṣṭi-, Av. mušti- 'fist'. Both of the branches Nourai draws (the fist word and mucrō) are therefore in Pokorny, but the fist branch carries Pokorny's own 'vielleicht'. Note that this page prints TWO homonymous roots called 'Meuk': this one (POK:745, 'scratch') and entry 1 ('stocking, boot'), which is in fact Pokorny's separate root meug-2, meuk- 'to slide, slip' on p. 744 — a reference Nourai does not give.",
      "note_fa": "ریشهٔ هندواروپایی آغازین *meuk- «خراشیدن، دریدن» در فرهنگ ریشه‌شناختی پوکورنی (ص ۷۴۵) به همین صورت آمده است؛ پس برچسب زبانی و شمارهٔ صفحهٔ نورایی درست است. تنها ایراد در معنی است: پوکورنی «kratzen, reißen» یعنی «خراشیدن، دریدن» آورده و نورایی «دریدن» را «مالیدن (rub)» ترجمه کرده است. مادهٔ اصلی این ریشه در یونانی ἀμύσσω «خراشیدن» و در لاتین mucrō «نوک تیز» است؛ پیوند «مشت» با این ریشه در خود پوکورنی با قید «شاید (vielleicht)» آمده است. توجه شود که در این صفحه دو ریشهٔ هم‌نام «Meuk» چاپ شده و ریشهٔ دوم (بند ۱) در حقیقت ریشهٔ جداگانهٔ meug-2/meuk- «لغزیدن، سُریدن» در ص ۷۴۴ پوکورنی است.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mucro",
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Reconstruction:Proto-Indo-Iranian/mu%C5%A1t%C3%AD%C5%A1"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {"ref": "POK:745", "status": "supports", "note": "POK_full.txt: 'Root / lemma: meuR- [OCR for meuk-] | Meaning: to scratch, tear | Material: Gr. ἀμυκαλαί … ἀμύσσω, Attic ἀμύττω \"split, scratch\", ἀμυχή \"crack, scratch\" …; Latin mucro \"a sharp point, edge, sword's point\"; perhaps to Old English ge-myscan …; perhaps also Old Indic musti-, Avestan musti- … | References: WP. II 255, WH. II 117 f. | Page(s): 745'. Exactly Nourai's root, page and material."}
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {"src": "WLD", "where": "Bd. II 255 (WLD_Bd2_pages.txt, s.v. 'meuk- „kratzen, ritzen\"')", "stance": "supports", "note": "'meuk- „kratzen, ritzen\". gr. ἀμυκαλαί … ἀμύσσω, att. ἀμύττω „ritze, zerkratze\" …; lat. mucro „scharfe Spitze … bes. des Speeres oder Schwertes\"; vielleicht lit. mušti „schlagen\"' — the root exists in Walde-Pokorny with the same content, but WP adds two escape clauses (see the node notes)."},
        {"src": "AHD", "where": "AHD_watkins1985.txt (Watkins, IE roots)", "stance": "silent", "note": "Watkins' list has no root 'meuk- to scratch'; the neighbouring entries are meug-1 'to act surreptitiously', meug-2 'slimy, slippery' [Pok. 2. meug- 744] and meuə- 'to push away' [Pok. 2. meu- 743]. Watkins simply does not recognise Pokorny's meuk- 745."},
        {"src": "IEC", "where": "Mann, s.v. mūkóo(n) (mūkró-) 'pike, spike, peak, pile' (IEC.txt line 11364, printed p. 814)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'mūkóo(n) (mūkró-) \"pike, spike, peak, pile\". WP II, 311 Per. mūk \"sting\"; Hes. mūkon \"pile\"; Lat. cf. (variant) mūcro … \"point of sword\"' — Mann keeps Lat. mucrō but under a noun-root 'spike', not under a verb 'scratch'; the fist word he puts under a separate 'mūk- \"beat, thrash, strike\"; muktis \"fist, blow\"' (line 11376)."}
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Avestan",
          "words": "mušti",
          "verdict": "disputed",
          "derivation": "Av. mušti- f. 'fist' is a real, well-attested word, but only inside the compound mušti.masah- 'of the size of a fist' (Bartholomae, Altiran. Wb. col. 1189: 'musti.masah- 1) n. \"Grösse einer Faust\"; 2) Adj. \"faustgross\": mušti.masaŋhəm xrūm \"a piece of raw meat the size of a fist\", Yt. 14.33'). What is disputed is the ARROW, i.e. its descent from a root *meuk- 'scratch': Pokorny himself only writes 'vielleicht auch ai. muṣṭi-, aw. mušti-', and Walde-Pokorny II 255 hedges twice over ('Lit. mušti könnte auch ursprgl. \"boxen\" gewesen sein; dann vielleicht zu ai. muṣṭi-ḥ \"Faust\"'), i.e. the fist word reaches *meuk- only through the Lithuanian verb mušti 'to strike'. Modern reconstruction takes the word back to Proto-Indo-Iranian *muštíš 'fist' < PIE *méws-ti-s ~ *mus-téy-s, from the root *mews- 'to shut, close (the hand)', with Tocharian B maśce 'fist' as the decisive comparandum; the derivation from *mewḱ- 'to scratch, tear' is expressly labelled dubious. Mann (IEC) likewise files Skt. muṣṭi- not under his 'spike' root but under 'mūk- \"beat, thrash, strike\"; muktis \"fist, blow\"'. So: the word and the Avestan label are right, the parent root is probably wrong.",
