          "derivation": "All eleven words are Latin-based and all go back to movēre or its participle mōtus. The inherited-looking one, move, came in through French: ME moven < Anglo-French mover / Old French movoir (Modern French mouvoir) < movēre, first attested late 13th c. The rest are learned or French borrowings of Latin derivatives: mobile < mobilis (< *movi-bilis) 'movable'; momentum < mōmentum (< *movi-mentum) 'movement, moment'; motion < mōtiōnem; motive < Late Latin mōtīvus; motor < mōtor 'mover'; remove < Old French removoir < removēre; remote < remōtus 'removed, far off'; promote < prōmōtus 'moved forward'; commotion < commōtiōnem (com- + movēre) 'violent movement, agitation'; emotion < French émotion < ēmovēre 'to move out, stir up'. Klein s.v. move cross-refers to exactly this set (mob, mobile, moment, motion, motive, motor, promote, remote, commove, remove) and Watkins' appendix prints the same list under meuə-. Nourai's list is a proper subset of Watkins' (he omits MOB, MOMENT(OUS), MOSSO, MOTIF, MOVEMENT), so nothing in it is unsupported.",
